r":"default","choices":[{"index":0,"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"length","native_finish_reason":"max_tokens","message":{"role":"assistant","content":null,"refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm planning to use Mockito to mock JdbcTemplate and ReflectionTestUtils to inject it along with setting the ustSatz field. For methods like getAuftrag, setzeStatus, neuePosition, and erstelleRechnung that rely on jdbcTemplate.query with RowMapper, I'll mock the query calls to return Auftrag objects directly rather than trying to test the RowMapper logic in detail.\n\nSince methods like getAuftrag make multiple query calls that return different data, I need to stub consecutive calls to return different lists in order—using Mockito's consecutive stubbing with doReturn chained multiple times to handle the first query returning one list and the second query returning another, working around generic type erasure by using unchecked casts.\n\nFor neuerAuftrag, there's a more complex chain: it calls queryForObject twice with different return types (Integer for the max count, Long for the insert ID), then update, then getAuftrag which itself makes two query calls—so I need to carefully mock each overloaded queryForObject variant separately using class type matchers to distinguish between Integer.class and Long.class calls.\n\nStubbing queryForObject... For varargs matching with Mockito, I need to handle the Object... parameter carefully—the standard approach is using any() to match the array, so I can stub with anyString(), eq(Long.class), and any() to capture the INSERT ... RETURNING calls. Mockito matchers for varargs are tricky since the actual invocation always passes arguments but matching zero varargs won't work. I need to use `Mockito.any(Object[].class)` to match the varargs array as a whole, or combine specific matchers like `anyString()` with `eq()` for the typed parameters and then handle the varargs portion separately. any(Object[].class) will match the entire varargs array regardless of how many arguments are passed. I need to handle the overloaded queryForObject method that doesn't use varargs separately by stubbing it without the third parameter. Now I'm planning out the test cases, starting with getAlleKunden and how to mock the JDBC template for it. For the vehicle tests, I'm following the same mocking pattern where jdbcTemplate.query returns canned lists directly, which means the RowMapper logic itself doesn't get exercised—but that's typical for unit tests with mocked JdbcTemplate since we're testing the query calls and data flow rather than the mapping implementation.\n\nTo get better behavioral coverage of the mapping logic including null handling for fields like baujahr and kmStand, I should capture the RowMapper argument and invoke it with a mocked ResultSet for at least a few methods like getKunde, getAlleFahrzeuge, mapAuftrag, and mapRechnung. It's more involved but worth doing to verify the mapping behavior, so I'll balance this by writing a moderate number of tests with ArgumentCaptor for the key ones.\n\nSetting up a helper method using Mockito's Answer to intercept the query calls...\n\nWriting test setup... Adding more test cases... For the update test, I need to stub the update method to return an int and verify the SQL contains \"UPDATE kunde\" without checking for queryForObject calls. Then for the delete test, I'm verifying that the update method is called with a DELETE statement targeting the specific kunde id. Moving into the Fahrzeug tests now, I'm setting up a RowMapper test that handles kunde name mapping when present in the ResultSet.\n\nSetting up the mock ResultSet with all the fahrzeug fields—id, kunde_id, kennzeichen, marke, modell, fahrgestellnr, baujahr, km_stand, pickerl_datum, angelegt_am—plus the kunde's nachname and vorname. Since wasNull() gets called after each numeric field retrieval, I need to make sure it consistently returns false throughout the test so no values are treated as null. Then I'm asserting that the kundeName combines the vorname and nachname correctly, and that baujahr and kmStand match the expected values.\n\nNext I'm testing the case where nachname is null, which means kundeName should remain null and not be set. For baujahr and kmStand, I'm simulating SQL NULL values by having wasNull() return true after those getInt calls, so those fields default to null since they're Integer wrappers. Then I'll test the scenario where a fahrzeug isn't found and the query returns null. After that, I'm testing the insert operation for a new fahrzeug, following the same pattern as the kunde insert test. I also need to verify the delete operation executes the correct SQL. Moving into the Auftrag tests now, starting with filtering by status—checking that the WHERE clause includes the status condition when it's provided, and handling the case when status is null or empty. I can use a doAnswer callback that inspects the SQL string to determine which RowMapper logic to invoke—if it contains \"FROM auftrag_position\", I'll apply the position mapping, otherwise the auftrag mapping. Setting up a counter to track sequential calls...\n\nNow I need to mock the ResultSet for mapping Auftrag objects.  The mock needs to return all the required fields—id, auftrag_nr, fahrzeug_id, kunde_id, status, beschreibung, km_stand, timestamps for angenommen_am, fertig_am, and abgeholt_am, plus the joined customer and vehicle data like nachname, vorname, kennzeichen, marke, and modell. Since different tests require different statuses (ANGENOMMEN, IN_ARBEIT, FERTIG, ABGEHOLT, STORNIERT) and getPositionen needs to return positions for price calculations, I'll create a helper method that builds a flexible mocked Auftrag ResultSet for the happy path, then adapt it as needed for each test scenario.\n\nFor the other methods like setzeStatus, neuePosition, erstelleRechnung, and neuerAuftrag, I'll stub the jdbcTemplate.query() calls directly to return pre-built Auftrag and AuftragPosition objects rather than testing the RowMapper itself—since these methods call getAuftrag() internally, I just need valid data to verify the business logic (status transitions, price calculations) without duplicating RowMapper tests that are already covered separately for getAlleKunden, getAlleFahrzeuge, and getAuftraege.\n\nNow I'm laying out the full test inventory: for KUNDE I'll test getAlleKunden with RowMapper execution to verify field mapping and SQL ordering, then sucheKunden...\n\nAdding test cases... 18. getAuftrag_found_setsPositionen tests the two-call flow where the RowMapper handles both the order and its positions, checking the null nachname branch and wasNull handling for km_stand. 19. getPositionen_returnsMappedPositions verifies position mapping via RowMapper. 20. neuerAuftrag_createsWithGeneratedNr_andUpdatesFahrzeugKm mocks the max order number query, the insert ID retrieval, updates the vehicle km, then retrieves the newly created order.\n\n21. neuerAuftrag_noKmStand_doesNotUpdateFahrzeug verifies that when kmStand is null, the fahrzeug update doesn't execute—I need to check that the single-argument update method (which takes the full SQL string built via concatenation) is never called with the UPDATE fahrzeug statement.\n\n22-26 cover status transitions: testing allowed transitions like ANGENOMMEN to IN_ARBEIT, disallowed ones that throw exceptions, missing aufträge, special cases like FERTIG to ABGEHOLT with the abgeholt_am timestamp, and the allowed FERTIG back to IN_ARBEIT path.\n\n27-28 test adding positions to open aufträge versus throwing when the auftrag is already abgeholt.\n\n29-30 cover position deletion and the case where adding a position fails because the auftrag doesn't exist.\n\nFor the RECHNUNG tests, I'm setting up comprehensive mocking: testing the RowMapper with null handling for nachname, verifying getRechnung returns null when not found, and for erstelleRechnung I'm using ReflectionTestUtils to inject the ustSatz, stubbing the auftrag query to return a FERTIG status with positions, mocking the count and max queries to return zero, stubbing the insert to return a new ID, then verifying the final getRechnung call returns the expected invoice with computed totals and tax. The last test ensures erstelleRechnung throws when the auftrag isn't FERTIG.\n\nThen I'm adding tests for when an invoice already exists, when the auftrag isn't found, and setzeBezahlt updates and returns the invoice.\n\nFor BERICHT, I'm testing the RowMapper execution, verifying getTopKunden returns the query result, and for getAdminStatistik I'm mocking multiple queryForObject calls and using an answer to inspect the SQL text to route different return values, plus checking the \"stand\" key matches a date format regex. The cleanup tests verify bereinigeStornierte deletes old records and returns the count, with a separate test for when nothing is found.\n\nThat's roughly 35-40 test methods covering the main behavioral paths. Now I'll start writing the test class with the necessary imports.\n\nSetting up the test class with MockitoExtension, mocking JdbcTemplate using ReflectionTestUtils to inject it since the class uses @Autowired on a private field, and setting the ustSatz default to 20. I need to import ResultSet, SQLException, Timestamp, Date, and use Mockito and AssertJ for assertions.\n\nWriting the test class setup... I'll apply lenient strictness to the MockitoExtension so shared stubs across multiple test paths don't trigger unnecessary stubbing exceptions.\n\nSetting up the test class... Strict stubbing detection with MockitoExtension applies globally across all mocks regardless of how they're created, so I need to be careful to only stub what each test actually uses to avoid UnnecessaryStubbingException. I'll stick with MockitoExtension and design each test carefully, then create a mapping helper for ResultSet mocks using when-thenReturn chains to handle the stubbing.\n\nWriting test code... Setting up the mock to return a list with the mapped customer object, then verifying the service retrieves all customers correctly and checking that the SQL query includes the proper ordering clause.\n\nFor the search test, I'm creating a scenario to verify how the service handles a search term that looks like a SQL injection attempt, checking whether it's being lowercased and embedded without proper escaping.\n\nNow moving on to the getKunde tests...\n\nI'm setting up test cases for when a customer is found versus not found, mocking the JDBC template to return either a single result or an empty list. Then I'm starting the speichereKunde tests for inserting new customers.\n\nAdding test assertions... Setting up the test for updating an existing customer, mocking the update and query calls to return the modified customer, then verifying the service returns the updated object and that the correct UPDATE statement was executed.\n\nNow I'm moving on to test the delete operation, which verifies that calling the service with a customer ID triggers the appropriate DELETE query. Then I'm starting the Fahrzeug tests, beginning with mocking a ResultSet to simulate database results for retrieving all vehicles with their associated customer information.\n\nSetting up the mock ResultSet with all the vehicle and customer data fields... Writing test assertions... Writing test cases... Setting up the mock to handle the row mapper invocation and return a list with the mapped result.\n\nNow I'm executing the service method and verifying the returned Auftrag object has the expected values—checking that null vorname gets converted to an empty string when concatenated with the nachname, and that the SQL query doesn't include a WHERE clause for status when it's null. Then I'm setting up a second test case to verify the behavior when a status filter is provided.\n\nFor the getAuftrag tests, I'm handling the not-found scenario by mocking an empty result and asserting null is returned, then working through the found case where I need to decide whether to use the RowMapper for full mapping or just return a pre-built Auftrag object since the mapping logic is already covered elsewhere.\n\nSince the mocked query method returns whatever we stub regardless of whether the RowMapper is actually invoked, I can simplify by directly stubbing sequential return values—first call returns the auftrag list, subsequent calls return the position list.\n\nNow I'm setting up the test cases for the business logic methods, starting with getAuftrag_found_setsPositionen by creating test data...\n\nSetting up test cases for getPositionen...","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm planning to use Mockito to mock JdbcTemplate and ReflectionTestUtils to inject it along with setting the ustSatz field. For methods like getAuftrag, setzeStatus, neuePosition, and erstelleRechnung that rely on jdbcTemplate.query with RowMapper, I'll mock the query calls to return Auftrag objects directly rather than trying to test the RowMapper logic in detail.\n\nSince methods like getAuftrag make multiple query calls that return different data, I need to stub consecutive calls to return different lists in order—using Mockito's consecutive stubbing with doReturn chained multiple times to handle the first query returning one list and the second query returning another, working around generic type erasure by using unchecked casts.\n\nFor neuerAuftrag, there's a more complex chain: it calls queryForObject twice with different return types (Integer for the max count, Long for the insert ID), then update, then getAuftrag which itself makes two query calls—so I need to carefully mock each overloaded queryForObject variant separately using class type matchers to distinguish between Integer.class and Long.class calls.\n\nStubbing queryForObject... For varargs matching with Mockito, I need to handle the Object... parameter carefully—the standard approach is using any() to match the array, so I can stub with anyString